/* ==============================================================
   03 — Pick and place: the full demo
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     Meca500 only (R3 or R4)
   TEACHES   Everything above, assembled into one cycle
   NEEDS     Robot activated + homed. Gripper optional — see the
             GRIPPER note below.
   WATCH     A four-position tray cycle that looks like a product
   TIME      ~60 s for the full cycle
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   WHAT THIS IS

   The demo to run when someone says "show me what it does". It
   picks four parts from a row and places them into a tray, and
   it is built entirely from the pieces in basic/ and
   intermediate/ — no new commands.

   Read it as a worked example of how the pieces fit:

     WRF          all coordinates are relative to the fixture,
                  z up, so moving the fixture is a one-line
                  change
     TRF          poses refer to the fingertips, z pointing
                  out through them — so every pose reads
                  180, 0, -90: fingers straight down
     SetConf      posture pinned, so every cycle is identical
     MovePose     for travel, where the path does not matter
     MoveLin      for the approach and retract, where it does
     MoveLinRelTrf   approach distance stated once
     Blending     on for travel, off at the work points
     Checkpoints  so a host knows where the robot actually is

   THE STRUCTURE

   Notice what this file does NOT have: a loop. Four picks means
   four blocks, written out. That is the honest consequence of a
   robot with no flow control, and it is worth showing rather
   than hiding — then explain that in production the host issues
   StartProgram("demo/pick") four times with a different variable
   each time, and the repetition disappears. See 01_variables and
   02_program_calls.

   WHAT THIS DEMO LEAVES OUT

   This program assumes every grip works. It closes the gripper,
   waits 0.8 s, and carries on to the tray — whether there is a
   part in the fingers or not. The robot will not object: closing
   on nothing is a perfectly successful GripperClose.

   A real cell checks. Close, ask the gripper whether it is
   holding a part, and only then carry on; if it is not, open,
   back off, go home, report the error and stop. The robot has
   no IF, so that check lives in the host — and the program has
   to be split at the grip to give the host the chance to make
   it.

   The demo stays in one piece because it has to run from the
   MecaPortal with nothing else connected. Its checkpoints 11,
   21, 31, 41 sit exactly where a host would be listening; what
   is missing is the other half of the conversation. IN A REAL
   CELL, at the bottom of this file, puts it back. Run this one;
   build that one.

   GRIPPER

   The gripper lines are commented out so this runs on a bare
   robot. If a gripper is fitted, uncomment them — search for
   GRIPPER. The Delay lines are already positioned correctly for
   the gripper's parallel execution (see intermediate/07).

   GEOMETRY

   Parts come from a row of four, 25 mm apart, and go into a 2x2
   tray 30 mm apart. Both are defined relative to the WRF, so if
   you have a real fixture, measure it once and change the single
   SetWrf line.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* ============ SETUP ========================================== */

/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode — so this
   program starts from firmware defaults no matter what ran
   before it. Reactivating brings the motors back.

   This does NOT move the arm: a robot that was already homed
   needs no re-homing. (With an MEGP 25* gripper fitted, the
   gripper re-homes on reactivation. The robot still does not
   move — but keep the fingers clear.)

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision and calibration config all
   survive. Run common/cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
Home()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


/* Start from a known state. Everything this program does not
   explicitly set — blending, payload, torque limits, move mode,
   posture selection — comes from here rather than from whatever
   ran before it.

   Save common/init.mxprog on the robot as "lib/init" first, or
   this reports [3017]. If you have not, comment the line out and
   uncomment the fallback block below it. */
StartProgram("lib/init")

/* --- Overrides for THIS program ------------------------------
   Everything below deliberately overrides what lib/init set.
   That is the pattern: init establishes the house defaults, the
   program states its own needs, and nothing is inherited by
   accident. */

// Speeds. Two of them: fast for travel, slow for process. Every
// tuning conversation about this program happens in these lines.
SetJointVel(30)
SetCartLinVel(250)          // travel
SetCartAngVel(180)

// Pin the arm posture so every cycle is identical. lib/init
// leaves automatic selection on, which is the safe general
// default; a production cycle wants it pinned.
//
// Front, elbow up — and wrist -1. With an MEGP 25E pointing its
// fingers down, the wrist bends down to do it, which is what
// the robot calls "flip" (joint 5 negative). Ask for +1 here and
// every pose below is refused.
SetConf(1, 1, -1)

// The tool: the house MEGP 25E from intermediate/02, with z out
// through the fingertips and x along the jaws. The 37 assumes
// 20 mm fingers — put in your real length.
SetTrf(37, 0, 14, 0, 90, 90)

// The fixture. Everything below is measured from here, so this
// is the only line that changes when the fixture moves. Its z
// points up, out of the fixture, like every WRF in this repo.
SetWrf(240, 0, 60, 0, 0, 0)

// GRIPPER: uncomment these two.
// SetGripperVel(40)
// SetGripperForce(40)

/* --- Fallback, if lib/init is not saved on the robot ---------
   Uncomment these if you commented out the StartProgram above. */
// SetAutoConfTurn(1)
// SetBlending(100)
// SetMoveMode(0)
// SetPayload(0, 0, 0, 0)
// SetTorqueLimits(100, 100, 100, 100, 100, 100)


/* ============ HOME ========================================== */

MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
MovePose(0, 0, 120, 180, 0, -90)       // safe height above fixture
SetCheckpoint(1)                       // "ready, tray may be loaded"

// GRIPPER: uncomment.
// GripperOpen()
// Delay(0.5)


/* ============ PART 1 ======================================== */
/* Pick from row position 1, place into tray cell 1.

   Read this block carefully — the three below are identical
   except for the two coordinate lines, and that repetition is
   the point being made in the header. */

// -- pick --
MovePose(-38, -35, 60, 180, 0, -90)    // above the part, blended
SetBlending(0)                         // arrive exactly at the part
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, 20, 0, 0, 0)       // descend along tool axis
Delay(0.3)
// GRIPPER: uncomment.
// GripperClose()
// Delay(0.8)
SetCheckpoint(11)                      // "grip step done" — NOT
                                       // proof that a part is held
// ^ In a real cell the program ENDS here. The host asks the
//   gripper whether it is holding a part, then starts either the
//   place program or the abort program. This demo cannot ask, so
//   it carries on regardless. See IN A REAL CELL, below.
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, -20, 0, 0, 0)      // retract along tool axis
SetBlending(100)

// -- place --
MovePose(38, -15, 60, 180, 0, -90)     // above the tray cell
SetBlending(0)
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, 20, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(0.3)
// GRIPPER: uncomment.
// GripperOpen()
// Delay(0.8)
SetCheckpoint(12)                      // "part 1 placed"
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, -20, 0, 0, 0)
SetBlending(100)


/* ============ PART 2 ======================================== */

MovePose(-38, -10, 60, 180, 0, -90)
SetBlending(0)
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, 20, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(0.3)
// GripperClose()
// Delay(0.8)
SetCheckpoint(21)
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, -20, 0, 0, 0)
SetBlending(100)

MovePose(38, 15, 60, 180, 0, -90)
SetBlending(0)
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, 20, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(0.3)
// GripperOpen()
// Delay(0.8)
SetCheckpoint(22)
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, -20, 0, 0, 0)
SetBlending(100)


/* ============ PART 3 ======================================== */

MovePose(-38, 15, 60, 180, 0, -90)
SetBlending(0)
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, 20, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(0.3)
// GripperClose()
// Delay(0.8)
SetCheckpoint(31)
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, -20, 0, 0, 0)
SetBlending(100)

MovePose(68, -15, 60, 180, 0, -90)
SetBlending(0)
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, 20, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(0.3)
// GripperOpen()
// Delay(0.8)
SetCheckpoint(32)
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, -20, 0, 0, 0)
SetBlending(100)


/* ============ PART 4 ======================================== */

MovePose(-38, 40, 60, 180, 0, -90)
SetBlending(0)
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, 20, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(0.3)
// GripperClose()
// Delay(0.8)
SetCheckpoint(41)
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, -20, 0, 0, 0)
SetBlending(100)

MovePose(68, 15, 60, 180, 0, -90)
SetBlending(0)
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, 20, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(0.3)
// GripperOpen()
// Delay(0.8)
SetCheckpoint(42)
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, -20, 0, 0, 0)
SetBlending(100)


/* ============ FINISH ======================================== */

MovePose(0, 0, 120, 180, 0, -90)
SetCheckpoint(99)                      // "cycle complete"

// Put everything back to defaults so the next example starts
// from a known state.
SetWrf(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
SetTrf(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
SetAutoConf(1)
SetCartLinVel(150)
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   IN A REAL CELL: THE GRIP HANDSHAKE

   Everything above runs open loop. This is what a production
   version of the same cycle does differently, and why.

   THE HANDSHAKE

       close the gripper
       ask: is it holding a part?
         yes   carry on to the tray
         no    open, back off, go home, report the error, stop

   The robot cannot run this — there is no IF. And it cannot be
   bolted onto the program above either: by the time the fingers
   close, the retract and the move to the tray are already
   queued behind them. The decision has to be made BEFORE the
   next motion is queued, not while it is running.

   So the program is split at the grip. The pick program ends
   with the arm down at the part and the fingers closing. With
   nothing left in its queue, the arm simply stays there. That
   pause is the host's window to decide.

   THE ROBOT PROGRAMS

   Three programs, saved on the robot (see 02_program_calls):

     cell/pick_1
         MovePose(-38, -35, 60, 180, 0, -90)
         SetBlending(0)
         MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, 20, 0, 0, 0)
         Delay(0.3)
         GripperClose()
         SetCheckpoint(11)            arm down, fingers closing

     cell/place_1
         the rest of the PART 1 block, from the retract on,
         unchanged

     cell/abort
         GripperOpen()                let go of whatever is there
         Delay(0.8)
         MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, -20, 0, 0, 0)
         MovePose(0, 0, 120, 180, 0, -90)
         MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
         SetCheckpoint(90)            "aborted, arm clear"

   All three rely on the SETUP section of this file having run
   first — the frames above all. Settings are sticky, so a cell
   runs its setup once, then calls these as often as it likes.

   The Delay after GripperClose is gone. It was a guess at how
   long the grip takes; the host now waits for the gripper to
   say so.

   One abort program serves all four pick points, because its
   retract is relative — straight back along the tool, wherever
   the tool happens to be. Note the order: open, retract, THEN go
   home. The arm is down at the fixture, and a MovePose straight
   home from there can swing through it. Opening first leaves a
   half-gripped part where it is, rather than carrying something
   nobody is sure of.

   THE HOST SIDE

       StartProgram("cell/pick_1")
       wait for [3030][11]           arm down, close started
       wait for the grip to finish   [2320] on the monitoring
         (see below), with a           port, or poll
         timeout                       GetRtGripperState()
       decide:
         held        StartProgram("cell/place_1"), carry on
         missed      StartProgram("cell/abort")
                     wait for [3030][90]
                     raise the alarm, stop the cycle
         timed out   a fault, not a miss: raise the alarm, and
                     leave the arm where it is until someone
                     has looked

   Wait for the checkpoint FIRST. Before it arrives the close may
   not have started, and the gripper state you read is left over
   from the last open.

   If [3040][11] arrives instead of [3030][11], the checkpoint was
   discarded — the robot stopped in error or on a safety signal —
   and the cycle is already over. That is the host's general
   error handling, not this handshake.

   What the operator does next — retry, skip that position, stop
   the batch — is a host decision, made on the HMI. The robot
   programs do not need to know.

   READING THE GRIPPER

       [2320][t, hp, dr, gc, go]

       hp   holding part      1 while the fingers are forcing
       dr   target reached    0 from the moment the close starts
                              until the fingers reach the
                              opening asked for
       gc   gripper closed    1 once fully closed
       go   gripper open      1 once fully open

   The host waits until hp = 1 (stopped on something) or dr = 1
   (got all the way to closed), then reads:

       held      hp = 1 and gc = 0   gripping something, and the
                                     fingers stopped short
       missed    gc = 1              closed all the way, so
                                     nothing is between them

   If you have changed SetGripperRange, check that its closed
   opening is smaller than the part, or a good grip reads as
   closed.

   For a stricter check, GetRtGripperPos returns the finger
   opening in mm. Compare it with the part's width and you also
   catch the wrong part, or a part gripped crooked. The manual
   quotes errors of up to 0.5 mm even with good fingers, so set
   the tolerance with that in mind.

   THE OTHER HANDSHAKES

   The grip is the one that matters most, but a real cell has a
   few more of the same shape:

     before the pick     is there a part? A sensor or a camera,
                         read by the host.
     before the place    still holding? Parts get lost in
                         transit. Same gripper check, same split.
     before the place    is the tray cell empty?
     after the release   did it let go? hp back to 0.

   WHAT IT COSTS

   Every split is a stop and a round trip to the host. That is
   cheap next to a dropped part, but it is not free, and on a
   fast cycle it shows. Put decision points where a wrong answer
   costs something. The grip always qualifies.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THINGS TO TRY

   1. Start at SetJointVel(30) and SetCartLinVel(250). Run it
      once at that speed so people can follow what is happening.

   2. Run it again at SetJointVel(100) and SetCartLinVel(1000).
      Same program, two edited numbers. This is the moment the
      cycle time argument gets made, and it makes itself.

   3. Have the log panel open. The checkpoint stream — 11, 12,
      21, 22, ... 99 — is what a PLC integrator will actually be
      looking at, and seeing it live is more useful to them than
      watching the arm.

   4. Change the single SetWrf line and run again. The whole
      cycle relocates. If you have a fixture you can physically
      move, this is the strongest thirty seconds in the demo.

   5. With a gripper fitted, take one part away — or run with no
      parts at all. The fingers close on nothing, the arm carries
      nothing to the tray, and every checkpoint still arrives on
      time. Show that, then walk through IN A REAL CELL. It is
      the most convincing way to explain why the host exists.

   QUESTIONS THIS WILL PROVOKE

   "Why is every part written out four times?"
      Because the robot has no loops. In production the host
      calls one saved program four times with different
      variables. Show advanced/01 and advanced/02.

   "What if the part isn't there?"
      The gripper tells you. An MEGP 25* reports whether it is
      holding a part, so the grip needs no extra sensor. The
      host reads that and decides: carry on, or abort, go home
      and raise the alarm. See IN A REAL CELL. Whether a part is
      waiting to be picked in the first place is a sensor or
      camera question — also the host's.

   "How accurate is the placement?"
      Note that blending is off at every work point, so the
      robot arrives exactly. Repeatability is a datasheet
      number — quote the datasheet, not the demo.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
